{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/pulls/480","id":4577841575,"node_id":"PR_kwDOGvVIyM8AAAABENxRpw","html_url":"https://github.com/actuallymentor/battery/pull/480","diff_url":"https://github.com/actuallymentor/battery/pull/480.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/actuallymentor/battery/pull/480.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/issues/480","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/pulls/480/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/pulls/480/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/issues/480/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/actuallymentor/battery/statuses/fe4ff1f2e5591295e40f50c56a47aa6f72a23156","number":480,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"🐛 Support macOS 27: use the macOS charge limit when the SMC charge keys are gone","user":{"login":"kariasamarth","id":7004302,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcwMDQzMDI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7004302?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth","html_url":"https://github.com/kariasamarth","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kariasamarth/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixes #477, addresses #474. Alternative to #469 / #478 / #479 that needs no new SMC keys, no adapter cut-off and no new privileged surface.\n\n## Problem\n\nOn macOS 27 (and the firmware shipped with the 26.7 / 15.8 updates) the SMC charge-inhibit keys are gone: `CHTE`, `CH0B`, `CH0C` read `no data`, and the replacement keys `bfD0/bfE0/bfF0` are entitlement-gated (`kIOReturnNotPrivileged`) even as root — on my M1 Pro they read `no data` too. `battery maintain` therefore never stopped charging and `battery status` said `smc charging unknown`.\n\n## Fix\n\nDetect a **charge control method** (`smc` / `native` / `none`) at startup. When no SMC charge key is present, use **macOS's own charge limit** (System Settings > Battery > Charge Limit) through PowerUIAgent's `PowerUISmartChargeClient`, reached via the stock JXA runtime (`osascript -l JavaScript`). This is the mechanism described in #474.\n\n- Works **without sudo**, no new binary, **`visudoconfig` is unchanged**.\n- The firmware enforces the limit, also during sleep and across reboots, and **drains a battery above the limit down to it by itself** (measured −1.5 to −1.9 A on AC after ~90 s), so `--force-discharge` is skipped on this path.\n- The `maintain_synchronous` loop on this path only re-asserts the limit every 60 s (e.g. if it was switched off in System Settings), so pidfile/daemon/GUI behaviour is unchanged.\n- The SMC path is untouched: `enable_charging`/`disable_charging` just gained a `native` branch; the two duplicated \"direct\" re-enable blocks in `disable_discharging` are factored into `allow_charging`.\n\nLimitations of the native method (all fail fast with a clear message, *before* a running maintenance is stopped):\n\n- the firmware refuses limits **below 80%** (`setMCLLimit:` segfaults below 80, so it is validated first) → `battery maintain 70` / `battery charge 50` exit 1\n- ranges are held at their **upper bound** (firmware decides hysteresis) → `battery maintain 70-80` holds at 80\n- **voltage** maintenance is not possible\n- `battery maintain stop` / `battery charging on` switch the macOS charge limit off, also if the user had set one themselves\n\nSub-80 limits *are* possible by writing `MCLFeatureState`/`mclLimitValue` into root's `com.apple.smartcharging.topoffprotection` domain (documented by the Ampere project, used by #479), but that needs root and therefore new sudoers entries — left out of this PR on purpose given the #469 review discussion. Happy to do a follow-up if wanted.\n\nAlso in this PR:\n\n- `get_voltage` always returned `0V`: `ioreg` now prefixes the line with tree characters, shifting the columns. Reads `$NF` (works on old output too). Same finding as #478.\n- `smc_read_hex` treats `Error: SMCReadKey() = …` like `no data` instead of returning the error text as a value.\n- `battery charging off` exits non-zero if charging could not be disabled.\n- README: requirements note + FAQ entry \"What changed on macOS 27?\"; help text notes.\n\n`BATTERY_CLI_VERSION` is **not bumped**, following the review on #469 (release is the maintainer's call). AGENTS.md says otherwise — happy to bump if preferred.\n\n## Testing\n\n**Hardware:** MacBook Pro 14\" M1 Pro (T6000), macOS 27.0 (26A428), battery at 100% on AC, CLI run from a copy with `binfolder`/`configfolder`/`daemon_path` pointed at a scratch dir.\n\nSMC probe on this machine:\n```\nCHTE/CH0B/CH0C/CH0I  no data      bfD0/bfE0/bfF0  no data\nCHIE                 (bytes 00)   CH0J            Error: SMCReadKey() = e00002c1\n```\n\nNative path (`charge control method: native` in the log):\n\n| Command | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `battery status` | `12.159V` (was `0V`), `smc charging disabled`, `macOS charge limit currently set to 80%` |\n| `battery status_csv` | `100,0:00,disabled,not discharging,70-80` |\n| `battery maintain 70-80 --force-discharge` | daemon logs \"Not triggering discharge…\", `pmset -g battlimit` → `chargeSocLimitSoc = 80` |\n| limit switched off externally while daemon runs | re-asserted within 60 s |\n| `battery maintain 85` | replaces daemon, limit 85 |\n| `battery charge 90` | stops maintenance, limit 90, completes (battery ≥ 90), `maintain recover` restores 85 |\n| `battery maintain recover` (launchd path) | recovers tracker value, sets limit |\n| `battery maintain stop` / `charging on` | `pmset -g battlimit` → `No battery level limits set`, `isMCLCurrentlyEnabled` = 0 |\n| `battery maintain 70`, `60-70`, `11.4V`, `charge 50` | exit 1 with explanation, nothing touched |\n| `setMCLLimit` 80…100 | 81, 85, 90, 95 accepted (any integer), 100 = no limit, <80 crashes → guarded |\n| drain | Amperage −1510…−1938 mA while limit registered, 0 mA after clearing |\n| `CHIE 08` (adapter cut) with native limit active | **ignored** — stays on AC for 30 s (firmware owns the power path while draining) |\n| `CHIE 08` with no native limit | works — `Now drawing from 'Battery Power'`, `ExternalConnected=No` within 5 s |\n| GUI \"Allow force-discharging\" toggle | daemon restarted with `--force-discharge`, skipped with log line, firmware drain continued |\n\nThe `CHIE` result is why `--force-discharge` is skipped on the native path rather than attempted: it would be a no-op while the limit is registered, and the firmware drains faster (~1.6 A) than an adapter cut does (idle draw). `battery discharge N` / `adapter off` / `calibrate` still work because they run `maintain stop` (clearing the limit) first.\n\nSMC path regression (fake `smc` emulating Tahoe `CHTE`/`CHIE`): `charge control method: smc`, `charging off` → `CHTE 01000000`, `charging on` → `00000000`, `maintain 60` at 100% → disables charging, `stop` → re-enables.\n\nNot tested: the GUI (Electron) end to end, macOS 26.7 / 15.8 machines (would need someone with that firmware to confirm `isMCLSupported`), `sudo smc -k ACLC -w` LED writes on macOS 27 (unchanged code, needs the installed sudoers file).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","labels":[],"m